SOLID Principles - Complete Interview & Design
Guide (Java 21 & Spring Boot)

      SOLID is a collection of five object-oriented design principles that help engineers build
      maintainable, testable, extensible and loosely coupled systems. These principles are heavily used
      in enterprise Java, Spring Boot applications, microservices, and system design interviews.

===========================================================================
S - Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
        
        -Definition: A class should have only one reason to change.

       - Benefits:
          • Easier maintenance
          • Better testability
          • Lower coupling
          • Higher cohesion

        -Java Example:
            class UserRepository {
            void save(User user) {}
            }
            class EmailService {
            void sendWelcomeEmail(User user) {}
            }
            class UserService {
            private final UserRepository repository;
            private final EmailService emailService;
            }

        -Interview Question:
            How do you identify SRP violations?
        -Answer: When a class handles multiple business concerns such as persistence,
        validation, notifications and reporting simultaneously.

==================================================
O - Open Closed Principle (OCP)
      
      -Definition: Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification.

      -Example:
        interface DiscountStrategy {
        double apply(double amount);
        }
        class PremiumDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {}
        class GoldDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {}

      -Adding a new discount requires a new implementation rather than changing existing code.

      -Interview Question:
          How does Strategy Pattern support OCP?
      -Answer: New behavior is added through new implementations.

====================================================================
L - Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

      -Definition: Derived classes should be replaceable for base classes.

      -Bad Example:
          Bird -> fly()
          Penguin extends Bird -> cannot fly
          Better Design:
          interface Bird {}
          interface FlyingBird extends Bird { void fly(); }

      -Interview Question:
          What happens when LSP is violated?
      -Answer: Runtime surprises and broken polymorphism.

============================================================
I - Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
    
    -Definition: Clients should not depend on methods they do not use.

    -Bad:
      interface Worker {
      work();
      eat();
      }
    -Better:
        interface Workable { work(); }
        interface Eatable { eat(); }

    -Interview Question:
      Difference between SRP and ISP?

    -Answer: SRP focuses on classes; ISP focuses on interfaces.
===============================================================

D - Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
  
      -Definition:
        High-level modules should depend on abstractions rather than concrete implementations.

      -Example:
          interface Database {
          void save(String data);
          }
          class MySqlDatabase implements Database {}
          class UserService {
          private final Database database;
          }

      -Benefits:
        • Mocking in unit tests
        • Loose coupling
        • Easier replacement of implementations
